steps.body:
Each component type gets a `ComponentArray<T>`: a dense `std::vector<T>` with no gaps, a parallel `owners` array saying which entity each slot belongs to, and a lookup from entity to slot.

```cpp
template <class T>
struct ComponentArray {
    std::vector<T> dense;                         // components, packed
    std::vector<Entity> owners;                   // owners[i] owns dense[i]
    std::unordered_map<Entity, std::size_t> indexOf;

    void add(Entity e, T value) {
        indexOf[e] = dense.size();
        dense.push_back(value);
        owners.push_back(e);
    }
    void remove(Entity e) {                       // swap-and-pop
        std::size_t i = indexOf[e];
        Entity last = owners.back();
        dense[i] = dense.back();
        owners[i] = last;
        dense.pop_back();
        owners.pop_back();
        indexOf[last] = i;
        indexOf.erase(e);
    }
};
```

Removing from the middle with `erase` would shift everything after it. Instead, **swap-and-pop** moves the *last* component into the hole and shrinks by one: O(1), and the array stays packed. The price is that order isn't preserved, and the lookup entry of the moved entity must be updated. (Many production ECS libraries, EnTT for one, use a plain array indexed by entity instead of a hash map: a *sparse set*.)

